C02_S03: UNIVERSAL REGULARIZED DICKE MIXTURE COMPILER
7 October UTC / 8 October Brisbane, 2026. Separate post-freeze addendum.
Root proposed uniform symmetric regularization; this worker reconstructed the
moment, positivity, error and bit arguments. C08_S03 proposed the rational pure
Bloch chart and dyadic sampling upgrade and independently checked its identity.
No previous proof, INITIAL, peer artifact, or implementation is edited.

THE PRECISE INTERFACE AND CLAIM
Let n>=1 and rho=sum_(k=0)^n pi_k |D_n,k><D_n,k|, pi>=0, sum pi=1.
PROMISE: there is a probability measure mu on [0,1] such that
 pi_k=integral binom(n,k)x^k(1-x)^(n-k) mu(dx).
This is equivalent to full separability for a state supported in the symmetric
space and diagonal in the Dicke basis. It is an input promise, not a membership
test. Positivity and symmetric support force every product vector in a pure
separable decomposition to have identical one-site factors; a common phase
twirl then gives precisely this binomial-measure representation. Conversely
the representation and phase averaging give a separable decomposition.

The input law is supplied either (a) as an EXACT RATIONAL normalized probability
vector of total bit length L, or (b) by an EXPLICIT charged acquisition procedure
which, on integer P, returns rational estimates of every pi_k with certified
absolute error <=2^-P and with stated bit/time cost A(n,L,P). No cost-free real
law, relative accuracy on tiny probabilities, or efficient acquisition of every
abstract computable law is asserted. In case (b), the compiler cost is A at
polynomial precision plus polynomial bit work. A polynomial total cost follows
only when the specified acquisition procedure has a polynomial cost bound.

For rational 0<epsilon<=1, put d=floor(n/2)+1 and M=d(n+1).
The compiler returns at most M pure iid n-qubit product labels and nonnegative
classical weights (zero-weight labels can be removed), with trace distance
<=epsilon from rho. Two output formats:

 I. Exact rational nodes/weights, exact algebraic one-qubit coherent descriptors
    using square roots and a primitive (n+1)st root of unity. Error <=epsilon/4.
 II. Exact rational 2x2 rank-one positive one-qubit density matrices, and common
     dyadic nonnegative weights summing to1. Error <=epsilon/2. Each classical
     label is sampled EXACTLY with a fixed polynomial number of fair bits.

All bit work and output lengths after acquisition are polynomial in n,L and
log(1/epsilon). The output is a positive, measurement-setting-independent
separable mixture. This is not a claim of the first polynomial classical local
measurement simulation, a dense 2^n output, physical preparation or priority.

1. REGULARIZATION MAKES THE CONDITIONING UNIFORM
Let sigma=P_sym/(n+1), eta=epsilon/8, and
 rho_r=(1-eta)rho+eta sigma.
The uniform coherent measure dx represents sigma, because each integrated
Bernstein basis function is 1/(n+1). Hence the TRUE representing measure
 mu_r=(1-eta)mu+eta dx >= eta dx.
The regularization error is D(rho,rho_r)<=eta, where D=(1/2)||.||_1.
This is valid even when mu is atomic or the original moment Gram is singular.
No model-dependent positive density, contour bound, strict pivot or endpoint
exception is used by the compiler.

2. ACQUIRE ORDINARY MOMENTS AGAINST THE TRUE REGULARIZED LAW
For 0<=ell<=n the exact ordinary x-moments are
 u_ell=sum_(k>=ell) binom(k,ell)/binom(n,ell) pi_k.
The binomial factorial identity proves this; u0=1. These are ordinary moments
derived from populations, not the incorrectly interpreted homogeneous/logit
quantities pi_k/binom(n,k).
Regularized moments are
 v_ell=(1-eta)u_ell+eta/(ell+1), v0=1.
All coefficients in the population-to-moment sum lie in [0,1]. Acquiring each
pi_k to zeta/[4(n+1)], then clipping the estimated u_ell to [0,1] and rounding
with absolute error <=zeta/4, gives moment errors <=zeta. Set v0=1 exactly.
The rounded population or moment vector NEED NOT itself have a positive
representing measure. Every matrix estimate below is compared with the TRUE
regularized moments and its known margin eta. This is the load-bearing distinction.

3. EXPLICIT RATIONAL GRAM MARGIN
Let H=(v_(i+j))_(0<=i,j<d). The true measure floor c=eta gives
 H>=c A0, A_r[i,j]=1/(i+j+r+1).
The Cauchy determinant identity and entries bounded by1 give, for r=0,1,2,
 det A_r=prod_(i<j)(j-i)^2 / prod_(i,j)(i+j+r+1),
 lambda_min(A_r)>=(4d)^-(d^2+d).
For the (1-x)-weighted Gram restrict to [0,1/2] and rescale; its lower bound
is 2^-2d times the same Hilbert margin. Thus one common bound is
 alpha=c*2^-2d*(4d)^-(d^2+d)>0.
It bounds H, the shifted Gram used below, and the (1-x)-weighted Gram.
log2(1/alpha)=O(log(1/epsilon)+n^2 log(n+1)), independently of the original law.

4. EXACT GAUSSIAN/RADAU RULE FROM THESE MOMENTS
For n=2m+1, use K=(v_(i+j+1))_(i,j=0..m).
For n=2m, write A=(v_(i+j+1))_(i,j=0..m-1), w_i=v_(i+m+1), and set
 K=[[A,w],[w^T,w^T A^-1 w]].
The even rule uses only moments through n and has rank m. The TRUE measure's
next moment has a nonnegative Schur complement, so replacing it by the minimal
Schur value gives H-K>=alpha I; K is PSD. In the odd case K>=alpha I and
H-K>=alpha I. Let H=LL^T and J=L^-1 K L^-T. Then 0<=J<I; the even J has
one eigenvalue0. Since L^T e0=e0 and K e_j=H e_(j+1) for j<m,
 J^j e0=L^T e_j for j=0..m.
Splitting ell=i+j proves e0^T J^ell e0=v_ell through 2m. In the odd case,
the final moment is (L^T e_m)^T J(L^T e_m)=v_(2m+1).
The Krylov vectors span, so the spectral measure of J at e0 has d nodes and
positive weights. The even rule is the Radau rule with prescribed endpoint0.
This construction covers n=1 and the regularized n=2 endpoint directly.

5. CHARGED MATRIX ACQUISITION AND EXACT POSITIVE OUTPUT
Choose
 tau<=epsilon/(16 n 3^n),
 zeta<=tau alpha^3/(256 d^3),
 e<=tau alpha^2/(32d).
Rounded moments give ||Hhat-H||<=d zeta. For odd n, ||Khat-K||<=d zeta.
For even n, use the EXACT rational minimal Schur completion of Khat; then
 ||Khat-K||<=10d^2 zeta/alpha^2.
The inverse perturbation identity proves this bound; the true top Gram has
margin alpha. These precisions ensure Hhat>0, Khat>=0 and Hhat-Khat>0.
In particular positivity of Khat is a property of the matrix construction,
not an assumption that the acquired moment vector lies in the moment cone.

Acquire a rational lower-triangular factor Lr within Frobenius error e of
chol(Hhat), with its first row (1,0,...) exact. Differentiating H=LL^T gives
 ||chol(Hhat)-L||_F<=3 d^(3/2) zeta/alpha.
The positive Cholesky pivots are >=sqrt(alpha/2). Interval integer square
roots/divisions acquire Lr with polynomial working bits: a sufficient coarse
error amplification bound for the finite recursion is (32d/alpha)^(2d^2).
This requires polynomial extra bits, not numerical magnitude polynomial in
1/alpha. Exact rational elimination/inversion has polynomial bit cost.

Set Jr=Lr^-1 Khat Lr^-T and M0=Jr/(1+tau). The constants above give
 ||Jr-J||<=64d^3 zeta/alpha^3+8d e/alpha^2<=tau/2,
 0<=M0<I, ||M0-J||<=3tau/2.
These are the already reconstructed algebraic bounds in the preserved
quadrature/BIT_COMPLEXITY_PROOF.txt, Sections8-9; only their prerequisite
measure floor has changed, from a model-dependent c to c=eta.

Apply the owned finite rational Jacobi algorithm to M0. It maintains exact
rational orthogonality and rotates the maximal off-diagonal pivot b. Dyadic
half-angle coordinates give an EXACT rational unit-circle rotation. Accept
only when the exact new pivot satisfies 2*bnew^2<=b^2. The squared off-diagonal
Frobenius norm then contracts by at least 1/[d(d-1)] of its value. At most
O(d^2 log(d/tau)) rotations give off-norm <=tau. Scalar working precision is
O(log(d/tau)); before stopping |b|>=tau/sqrt(d(d-1)), so the exact acceptance
test succeeds at that precision. The d=1 case has no rotations.

Let Q be the exact rational orthogonal matrix and T=Q^T M0 Q. Output nodes
 x_i=T_ii in [0,1], weights w_i=Q_(0i)^2>=0, sum w_i=1.
For even n, replace the minimum node by exactly0. Its value is <=tau because
M0 has an eigenvalue0 and ||T-diag(T)||<=tau. The represented contraction
 Jout=Q diag(x_i)Q^T satisfies ||Jout-J||<=4tau.
Rational bit lengths grow at most additively over the polynomial number of
rotations: each accepted rotation's common denominator has O(log(d/tau))
bits, and the initial rational matrix has polynomial bit length. No eigenvalue
gap, tiny true quadrature weight, or approximate-eigensolver promise is used.

6. STATE ERROR AND ALGEBRAIC LABELS
Power telescoping for contractions gives moment error <=4 ell tau. The sum
of absolute coefficients in all Bernstein polynomials of degree n is 3^n.
Therefore population L1 error <=4 n 3^n tau and Dicke-state trace distance
 D(rho_r,rho_quad)<=2 n 3^n tau<=epsilon/8.
For each node use phi_(i,j)=sqrt(1-x_i)|0>+zeta^j sqrt(x_i)|1>,
 j=0..n, zeta=exp[2pi i/(n+1)] of EXACT order n+1, with weight w_i/(n+1).
This finite phase sum kills every off-diagonal Dicke entry exactly. Its M
labels are pure iid products. Format I has error <=eta+epsilon/8=epsilon/4.
Its roots and root-of-unity descriptors have finite polynomial bit length.

7. RATIONAL PURE LOCAL DENSITIES AND BOUNDED FAIR-BIT SAMPLING
For each format-I factor let b=(b_x,b_y,z) be its unit Bloch vector, with
 z=1-2x, b_x=2sqrt(x(1-x))cos(theta), b_y=2sqrt(x(1-x))sin(theta).
Choose chart sign s=+1 when z>=0 and s=-1 otherwise, and
 t=(b_x,b_y)/(1+|z|).
Acquire and round each coordinate to a dyadic vector t_hat with
 ||t_hat-t||_2<=epsilon/(8n). Absolute integer-square-root intervals acquire
sqrt(x(1-x)); no relative division by tiny x is used. The chart denominator
is >=1. Phases can be acquired with the geometric Machin arctangent series
for pi and bounded-angle sine/cosine Taylor series, in polynomial bit work.
All angles lie in [0,2pi]. Exact rational x fixes the chart sign.

Define the RATIONAL exact unit vector
 B_s(t_hat)=(2t_x,2t_y,s(1-||t_hat||^2))/(1+||t_hat||^2).
Its density (I+B dot sigma)/2 is trace1, rank1 and PSD EXACTLY. The identity
 1-B_s(t) dot B_s(u)=2||t-u||^2/[(1+||t||^2)(1+||u||^2)]
implies single-qubit D<=||t-u||_2, even when ||t_hat||>1. Tensor telescoping
and mixture convexity give at most epsilon/8 additional state error. All
factors within each n-qubit label remain identical.

Choose a common Qden=2^b>=8M/epsilon. Floor the first M-1 atom weights to
multiples of 1/Qden; give the last atom the remaining mass. Weights remain
nonnegative and sum1 exactly; the classical TV error is <M/Qden<=epsilon/8.
The induced state error is no larger. Uniformly sample b fair bits and locate
the resulting integer in the cumulative integer-weight intervals. This gives
EXACT bounded-bit classical label sampling without rejection or an exact-real
randomness assumption. It does not implement preparation of the selected qubit.
Total format-II error <=4*(epsilon/8)=epsilon/2<epsilon.

8. ACQUISITION COROLLARY FOR RATIONAL FINITE EXPONENTS
If pi_k=exp(r_k)/sum_j exp(r_j), with an explicitly supplied polynomial-size
list of binary rational r_k, the required absolute acquisition is polynomial
even when some |r_k| is exponentially large as a NUMBER. Subtract r_max,
write x_k=r_max-r_k>=0, and choose an integer cutoff C=P+O(log(n+1)).
For x_k>=C, use0 with error <=2^-C, since e>2. For x_k<C, the positive
Taylor/reciprocal interval scheme in the frozen proof acquires e^-x_k to
2^-C using O(C) terms and polynomial bit arithmetic. The denominator is
>=1 because a maximum exponent contributes exactly1. Positive normalization
charges only a factor O(n+1) in absolute error. This is an absolute probability
certificate; no relative rare-population or giant exp(h) evaluation is claimed.

Thus the symmetric Dicke Gaussian with binary rational delta,h has such an
acquisition routine for ANY magnitudes of those rational inputs. Applying the
compiler still requires its separability promise. That promise on the sharp
domain is a SEPARATE imported exact theorem, pinned in OPTIMAL_DOMAIN_EXTENSION.

9. ACTUAL EXECUTION SCOPE AND PRESERVATION
The new owned atomic_fixture.py uses the frozen owned rational Jacobi core and
an exact rational posterior check on two regularized atomic laws: n2 endpoint
mu=delta_(1/2), and n6 mu=(1/5)delta0+(1/2)delta_(1/3)+(3/10)delta1.
The original Grams have ranks1<2 and3<4, respectively. Both regularized rules
PASS exact positivity, sum-one, support, endpoint0 and population-error checks.
Their total admitted campaign cost, including a preserved serialization-only
failed attempt and repaired replay, is below30 seconds. This is finite runnable
evidence for the boundary interface, not execution of every law or a physical
sampler. The format-II pure-density/sampling rounding is proved here but is
not claimed executed by these population fixtures.

The prior c03_s01 practical implementation's tested delta<=1 scope remains
unchanged. The universal theorem is an explicit finite-bit construction; the
new two-fixture frontend does not relabel that prior implementation or imply
an acquired unrestricted whole-Gibbs ensemble. Whole-space sector/subset
acquisition and its costs remain separate c01_s03/root results.
